{"page_content": "Solid-solid separation\n\nRFair= release of dusts and volatile substances Substance in solid waste Substance in solid waste Separation efficiency Substance in solid waste RFwater = release to water in case separation involves water\n\nFurther treatment as \u2022Municipal waste \u2022Material recycling waste \u2022Hazardous waste\n\nFurther treatment as \u2022Municipal waste \u2022Material recycling waste \u2022Hazardous waste\n\nFigure R.18- 10: Distribution scheme for separation techniques applied to solid dry wastes\n\n1\n\nSolid-liquid separation techniques\n\nExamples for these separation techniques are filtration, sorption, flotation, sedimentation and centrifugation. Separation of these phases can be facilitated by chemical treatment (flocculation or pH variations of the waste). Separation is mainly carried out to concentrate components of the waste of concern in one of the phases in order to subject both phases to further treatment in a more efficient way. Two cases can be distinguished which influence the phase into which a substance is separated:\n\na) Substance is not bound to waste matrix/particles\n\nIf the assessed substance is not integrated into a matrix, its distribution depends on its substance properties. These substance properties may enhance each other, resulting in the substance unambiguously being distributed to only one of the phases or may counteract, resulting in more complex relations determining in which phase the substance will end up. The most relevant properties determining distribution are:\n\nLogKow: with increasing LogKow, the substance concentrates in the solid phase. Other indicators of hydrophobicity can be used as well. Also the Koc as indicator for the tendency of substances to sorb to organic matter may be used.\n\nSolubility: the more soluble the substance is, the higher is its percentage in a water phase", "metadata": {"doc_id": "85151928-7221-4492-a8a2-4e82e79dc92a", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "r18_v2_final_en.pdf"}}